d has no --fault, so it would write a clean condition with no injected fault. I added --fault tool_timeout:reserve:0 because the sample README asks for it. The main README's example says `--fault tool_timeout:reserve` without the occurrence, so I was not sure which form is canonical. | Every link in the README points to github.com, not to local relative paths, so a reader with the checkout cannot tell which local docs/ files are 'linked'. I did not open any docs/ file. | The sample README links ../../../docs/measurement/ttfv-protocol.md (off limits; not opened). It also says 'Until agent-reliability-ci is on PyPI', which contradicts the main README saying v0.8.0 is on PyPI. | The demo's 'ok' lines and the 'All six steps held' ending are clear. The relationship between alpha/(4K) in STATISTICS and the '97.5%' per-arm confidence shown in the table was not obvious to me as a newcomer. | The README talks about a 'System One decision endpoint (TypeSafe's Jev API)' with no explanation. For a first-time visitor it is unclear whether that is relevant to a normal Python agent (I concluded it is not). | uv chose CPython 3.13.12 for the venv, although the environment notes say Python 3.14 is on PATH. It worked, but I did not choose it. Reader notes: Timing: README opened 12:05:40Z, and the demo started 12:05:44Z. The banner appeared at 12:05:45Z (uvx resolved quickly, so the package seemed cached already), the B verdict at 12:06:09Z, and 'All six steps held.' at 12:06:58Z (about 74 s). Track B's install took about 1 s (packages were in uv's cache: 'Installed 7 packages in 7ms'), so this is not a cold-network install time. Scaffold took under 1 s; the run took about 2 s (12:07:33Z to 12:07:35Z). From README opened to the validated gate verdict was about 2 minutes (12:05:40Z to 12:07:40Z), including the demo. My time was inflated by the blocked install and the failed first init, but I read fast, so a human would take longer to read the README. After both demo runs, load average 