pen docs/STATISTICS.md or other linked docs, so I could not check them. | The README's instruction to pipe through PIPESTATUS did not capture the exit code in this shell (zsh), so the demo exit code is missing. | The sample project's README says its timing is measured by the TTFV protocol under docs/measurement, which I was not allowed to open. I do not know what that protocol is. | I did not know what 'fault' versus 'condition' meant in the output, or what 'ok' lines in the demo asserted. Reader notes: Track A: readme_opened 12:21:51Z; demo ran 12:21:58Z to 12:23:13Z (about 75 s), all six steps logged. Its own inner outputs: A vs B BLOCK (exit 1), A vs A PASS (exit 0), A vs C PASS (exit 0). The demo's own lines say the counts matched the recorded run. Track B: the copy is at <tmp>/python_agent with the venv at .../<tmp>/venv. The smoke run took about 2 s wall time. The gate reran on the same store and printed the same verdict; the decision.json hash was the same before and after. verdict_printed has no per-line timestamp: I stamped only the time the arci run command returned (12:23:56Z), so the VERDICT line appeared at or before that time. Machine state: macOS (darwin 27), Homebrew uv 0.12.5, Python 3.14.6 from mise, zsh shell. I did not run ps or check load, so I cannot say whether other processes were running. I did not read any docs/ file, nor docs/measurement/ or plan/, and did not read src/, tests/, bench/ or examples/ beyond copying the sample project and reading its README and generated outputs.\"}. Scorer rationale: All five answers contain every required element. q1: baseline vs candidate, repeated runs on the same predeclared tasks and injected tool faults, verdict with exit code naming PASS and BLOCK. q2: people changing an agent who want to know if it got less reliable, and the agent must be a function agent(task, tools, rng) whose tool calls go through the tools object (the key allows the function or the MCP route). q3: relative to the baseline, drop s